<Project>

    <!-- What this package asks of a consumer's build, and the whole of it: nothing, on a compiler that can
         load the analyzer, and the analyzer taken back out on one that cannot.

         The same file is packed to buildTransitive/ and to build/, so a project can end up importing both
         copies. Nothing here may depend on running once. It defines a target and nothing else — no
         property, no item, no import — so a second import redefines that target with identical text and
         leaves exactly what one import left. That emptiness is also what makes the file safe for the
         consumers who get it without ever asking for it: buildTransitive/ flows through a package
         dependency, so a project that references only SlideRule.Xunit imports this file while
         receiving none of this package's analyzers. The removal below then matches no item and does
         nothing, which is the right answer for them.

         A compiler below Roslyn 4.0.1 cannot load the analyzer and says so with CS8032 on every build of
         every project that references this package: a warning ordinarily, an error wherever warnings are
         errors, and an estate that has pinned an old SDK for years is often one that builds with
         warnings as errors. Taking the analyzer back out is what leaves such a build working exactly as
         it did before this package carried one. Measured on the 3.1 and 5.0 SDKs: CompilerApiVersion is empty on a host below the
         floor and carries a value on every host at or above it, and the Analyzer items carry
         NuGetPackageId on all of them.

         The removal is by NuGetPackageId and never by file name, so it takes out everything this package
         put in the directory: the spec-literal analyzer, the completion provider beside it and the copy
         of the contract they load against. A third file joining the closure needs no edit here.

         The target's NAME is significant and deliberately not the one the analyzer package uses. A spec
         project can reference both packages; MSBuild lets the later definition of a target name replace
         the earlier one silently, so two packages sharing a name would leave one package's analyzers in
         place below the floor and the CS8032 back. The two package ids are compared with ==, not by
         prefix, so 'SlideRule' and 'SlideRule.Analyzers' select their own items and
         nothing of each other's. -->
    <Target Name="_SlideRuleRemoveContractAnalyzerBelowTheFloor"
            BeforeTargets="CoreCompile"
            Condition="'$(CompilerApiVersion)' == ''">
        <ItemGroup>
            <_SlideRuleContractAnalyzerBelowTheFloor Include="@(Analyzer)"
                                                      Condition="'%(Analyzer.NuGetPackageId)' == 'SlideRule'" />
            <Analyzer Remove="@(_SlideRuleContractAnalyzerBelowTheFloor)" />
        </ItemGroup>
    </Target>

</Project>
